However, in lojban, does not the absence of an argument to a selbri simply
mean that argument is unspecified?  In such case, "prami mi" means "Someone
unspecified Loves Me" and is not the command "Love Me".  In order to make it
a command, if I recall correctly, One is *required* to use "ko", so as to
make the statement, "You (imperative), Love Me."  Such an idiom ("absence
equals unspecified") is seen in the former Google Wave lesson where the
Student is instructed the phrase "zdani mi" means "something houses Me" or
"I have a house" and is not told "You (imperative), house Me."  Is there
actually an updated version of lojban, this "modern lojban", I have missed?
 Or are You just saying, "If We were to start from scratch, We might make
'broda' equal 'ko broda'."?

Just wanting to make sure I have read materials correctly.

mi'e xuinkrbin.

